{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>From the turtledoves or from the young of pigeons.</b> Scripture chose these two species because they are readily available and easy to catch, as our Rabbis mentioned (Leviticus Rabbah 27:6): “A lamb of the sheep or a lamb of the goats” — one should bring from what is raised in one's stall, and not need to take one's weapons, his pouch and his bow, to hunt game to bring. But He wanted the adult turtledoves because of their devotion and attachment, as is well known of them, that once its mate is lost it will never attach itself to another; and so Israel are attached to HaShem their Elohim and will never attach themselves to another el forever. But pigeons are extremely zealous, and in their zeal they will separate and exchange mates; therefore He chose from among them only the young, before they pair up, for the pigeon in its youth develops love for its rearing nest more than other birds. And our Rabbis mentioned (Exodus Rabbah 42:9) that all birds, if a person touches their nest to take fledglings or eggs from there, will abandon it and never nest there again; but the pigeon will not abandon it under any circumstances; and so Israel will never exchange their Creator and His Torah forever, but rather either Jew or crucified. And He did not choose chickens because of their promiscuity.\n\nAnd the Rav said in the Guide of the Perplexed (III:46) that the reason for offering adult turtledoves and fledgling pigeons is that this is the best among them, because the adult pigeon is not palatable. And this is not true, for young pigeons are hardly fit for eating because of the excess moisture in them. But if we consider their natural disposition in eating, it is possible that they were chosen for their innate property: the innate property of turtledoves is sharpening the intellect, and the innate property of pigeon fledglings is great benefit for diseased organs, such as *pallas* and the like."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The phrase 'או יהודי או צלוב' (either Jew or crucified) appears to be a paraphrase of the midrashic dictum in Exodus Rabbah; rendered hyper-literally as it stands in the text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:39.375353970Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}